WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.444
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.678 --> 00:00:10.220
STEPHANIE WONG: You can't really
talk about storage at Google

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:13.490
without talking about how
our production systems work.

00:00:13.490 --> 00:00:15.440
How are all the
hard disks and flash

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.930
drives on individual
machines connected?

00:00:17.930 --> 00:00:19.190
Let's boil it down.

00:00:19.190 --> 00:00:22.920
Data is stored in hard disk
drives or solid state drives.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:25.280
SSDs and HDDs are
deployed separately

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:26.720
from the applications.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:29.460
For example, they can be
stored in a dedicated storage

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.980
tray like this one.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.100
In other words, we separate
Storage from Compute.

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:36.050
Data needed by a
machine is typically

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:38.450
not even in the same
rack because machines

00:00:38.450 --> 00:00:41.090
can access data in a
different physical location

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:44.180
through our robust
global fiber network.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:46.370
Splitting Storage and
Compute lets us scale them

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:48.410
independently as demand grows.

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.980
And machines can process
requests much more efficiently.

00:00:51.980 --> 00:00:53.870
We organize Storage
and Compute racks

00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.060
into rows of
physical enclosures,

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:58.730
organizing groups of
enclosures into clusters,

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:02.630
and putting multiple clusters
in a single data center.

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.990
Each cluster depends on their
own power, cooling, and network

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:07.790
infrastructure,
a deliberate part

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:10.670
of how we design for data
protection and reliability

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:12.300
at scale.

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.990
We've built our own
warehouse-scale machine out

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.540
of hundreds of thousands
of relatively inexpensive

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:18.680
machines.

00:01:18.680 --> 00:01:21.410
At Google, it's rare to
dedicate an entire storage

00:01:21.410 --> 00:01:24.350
appliance in our data
center for storing only one

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.970
product or service's data.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.630
Instead, we spread a workload's
data across multiple machines,

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:32.630
and workloads share network
access to that storage.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:35.240
Encryption is inherent
in our storage systems,

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.960
and all data is encrypted
prior to being written to disk.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.440
But how does Google make
storage accessible and scalable

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:44.840
across a global
fleet of machines?

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.940
Remember, at
planetary scale, it's

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:49.970
not unusual for individual
machines, racks,

00:01:49.970 --> 00:01:52.760
or even entire buildings
to periodically fail,

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.670
so we need to build software
to make the data stored on them

00:01:55.670 --> 00:01:56.600
durable.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:59.630
That way, when they
fail, no data is lost.

00:01:59.630 --> 00:02:01.550
Every layer of
our storage stack,

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:04.640
down to the file system and the
layer that writes to storage

00:02:04.640 --> 00:02:07.700
devices, is a shared service.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:10.460
Services like Search,
Photos, and Gmail

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:13.440
workloads share machines
at the data centers.

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:16.580
Resources within each machine,
like Compute and Memory,

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:19.010
are allocated to each service.

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:22.350
At a machine level,
disk, or D for short,

00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:25.130
is a system that exposes
the hard disk and SSD drives

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:27.950
attached to individual
machines to other services

00:02:27.950 --> 00:02:29.070
in the cluster.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:31.850
It manages access to each
machine's disk capacity

00:02:31.850 --> 00:02:34.100
to maximize utilization.

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:36.560
At the cluster level,
most data we store

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:39.320
has high durability and
latency requirements,

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:43.700
so we built a file system called
Colossus on top of D, which

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.790
is the foundation for many
services like Cloud Storage

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:48.140
and Bigtable.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:50.690
At the Colossus level,
files are broken down

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.750
into a set of chunks that can
be stored on different machines

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:55.110
in the cluster.

00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.350
This data replication
across machines

00:02:57.350 --> 00:02:59.930
is the key to fast
recovery and fault

00:02:59.930 --> 00:03:03.350
tolerance against things
like network failures.

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:06.230
For a given chunk, Colossus
identifies a machine

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:08.990
to write the chunk to, and
the client sends the chunk

00:03:08.990 --> 00:03:11.450
to the D service that
runs on the target machine

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.070
to perform the write.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:16.250
Security is ensured through
the encryption of each chunk,

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:19.580
and each chunk has a
unique encryption key.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:22.640
Because a single machine can
be running multiple services,

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.480
and conversely, a service can
be running on many machines,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:28.760
services are constantly
adapting to the amount

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.170
of resources they use.

00:03:30.170 --> 00:03:32.990
Now you can see how distributed
data better utilizes

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:36.380
our machine capacity and gives
Google Cloud Services higher

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.330
reliability and performance.

00:03:38.330 --> 00:03:40.640
For Google Cloud users,
you have the option

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.640
to store data on local
solid state drives,

00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:46.880
zonal persistent disks that
map to clusters across zones

00:03:46.880 --> 00:03:50.000
in the same region, or
regional persistent disks

00:03:50.000 --> 00:03:52.790
across cloud regions or
even storage buckets.

00:03:52.790 --> 00:03:55.700
You can store data in a single
region for high performance,

00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:58.670
dual region for high performance
and high availability,

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.400
and multiple regions for
the highest availability.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.290
Your data maps to
different clusters

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.350
and protects it from zonal
and regional failures.

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:10.490
D and Colossus manage access to
the storage, store data safely

00:04:10.490 --> 00:04:13.430
and securely, and
efficiently use our hardware

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.950
so you get the best
performance possible.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:18.500
All of this is possible because
of the speed of our network

00:04:18.500 --> 00:04:19.430
fabric.

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:22.129
More on that next time on
Discovering Data Centers.

00:04:22.129 --> 00:04:25.479
[MUSIC PLAYING]

